      "text": "AVL Trees (Adelson - Velskii - Landos Trees)",
      "fontSize": 38.70221625434027,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AVL Trees (Adelson - Velskii - Landos Trees)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "mB5_CQ7dGnWonmFoGbji2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 328.9597733037738,
      "y": 228.452357474559,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#c2255c",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVy",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1565715554,
      "version": 903,
      "versionNonce": 943445054,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1753859634348,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AVL Trees are Self-Balancing Binary Trees as in for every node of AVL tree is balanced \nThe major difference is how ?? and when ?? the AVL tree Balances a Node :-\n\n1] AVL Trees balance a node after insertion of a node at a particular position causes a part\n   of the subtree to become unbalanced.\n\n2] Depending upon the position of insertion of new node that causes imbalance in the tree there\n   are specific rotations (LL , RR ,RL , LR ) in order to make the tree balance again.\n\n3] So over all balancing a tree is not complex task as it is important to undertand that we only \n   balance an imbalance subtree and once that subtree is properly balanced we don't have to change \n   the structure of the remaining tree ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AVL Trees are Self-Balancing Binary Trees as in for every node of AVL tree is balanced \nThe major difference is how ?? and when ?? the AVL tree Balances a Node :-\n\n1] AVL Trees balance a node after insertion of a node at a particular position causes a part\n   of the subtree to become unbalanced.\n\n2] Depending upon the position of insertion of new node that causes imbalance in the tree there\n   are specific rotations (LL , RR ,RL , LR ) in order to make the tree balance again.\n\n3] So over all balancing a tree is not complex task as it is important to undertand that we only \n   balance an imbalance subtree and once that subtree is properly balanced we don't have to change \n   the structure of the remaining tree ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
